v4.5.8
Radon Player 4.5.8: Ignore Bluetooth devices always plays on (no stutter at all with Shizuku), new music from your taste, and battery
**Install or update:** `RadonPlayer-Next.apk` (Android), `Radon-Player-4.5.8.msi` (Windows), `radon-player_4.5.8_amd64.deb` (Linux). Each installs over the version you have and keeps your settings and stats.

**What is new**
- **Ignore Bluetooth devices: the music always carries on (the phone):** cars and earbuds send a pause of their own as an ignored device connects or disconnects (the car as it comes or lets go, Android Auto as it ends, the earbuds as the car takes the sound), which looked just like you pressing pause, so the music sometimes stayed stopped until you pressed Play. A pause like that, in the seconds around an ignored device coming or going, is now left alone or undone, as long as there's somewhere other than the phone's speaker to play. And when your earbuds drop out as the car connects, the app waits up to twenty seconds for them (it was four) and plays on as soon as they're back.
- **Less stutter as Android Auto starts:** the app no longer lets go of your earbuds and takes them back in the same second while Android Auto sets itself up (each change was a small gap). The setting now says plainly that going in and out of range of an ignored device can stutter for a moment, but the music always carries on by itself.
- **No stutter at all, with Shizuku (the phone, Android 14 or newer):** a new switch under Ignore Bluetooth devices. With Shizuku (a free app) running, an ignored device has its Media audio switched off while your headphones are connected, so it never takes the sound; with no headphones for a few seconds it gets it back, so the car still plays the music when it's all there is. Calls and Android Auto are untouched. Settings walks you through setting Shizuku up, and the switch takes you to whatever step is next (installing it, starting it, allowing the app). Switching it off gives Media audio back.
- **The DJ's new music picked from your taste:** new songs start from the songs you love most (played to the end, favourites, More like this, repeats, artists you've just fallen for), not only the latest ones. Artists suggested by several of your favourites come first, and ones you've drifted from don't lead anywhere. What you said no to, songs you skip early and artists whose new songs you keep skipping are left out, and the rest is ranked against your taste. Every set is put in the order you tend to play things (what you usually play after what), still never the same artist twice in a row. Discovery playlists and New releases follow the same picks.
- **Easier on the battery:** with the app off the screen, or the music paused, the app's timers wake the phone far less often (the visualizer, the lyrics, the clock, the search for your PC, Sync's check, the remote), and Wi-Fi is kept fully awake only when the music needs the network. Nothing you see or hear changes, and nothing waits when you come back.
- **Tidier inside:** code nothing used any more is gone (an old Now playing screen among it), so the app is a little lighter.
